  "text": " colorWith the formula 2a² +h². Let's denote the foci as \\( F_1 \\) and \\( F_2 \\). To find the distance \\( 2a \\), we can use the formula \\( 2a^2 = 80^2 + h^2 \\) where \\( h \\) is the distance between the center of the ellipse (midpoint of CD) and the midpoints of AB, AD, and BC, which is the same as the x-coordinate of the center (since \\( h \\) is perpendicular to the midpoints of the parallel sides, which all coincide at the y-axis due to the symmetry of this parallelogram).\n\nUsing the Pythagorean theorem, we know that:\n\n\\[ 2a^2 = 80^2 + h^2 \\]\n\nSince AB = CD = 80 and AD = BC = 120, and remembering that h is the distance from the y-axis to the line containing AB and CD (which is 80/2 = 40 since these lines are directly parallel to each other).\n\nWe can now plug in the values:\n\n\\[ 2a^2 = 80^2 + 40^2 \\]\n\\[ 2a^2 = 6400 + 1600 \\]\n\\[ 2a^2 = 8000 \\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[ 2a = \\sqrt{8000} \\]\n\\[ 2a = 40\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\nThis is approximately 89.44, but since we need an exact formula, our answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{40\\sqrt{5}} \\]",
